      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The official vector definition confirms v65201210 is Canada's all-industries GDP at basic prices, seasonally adjusted at annual rates, in chained 2017 dollars, from Table 36-10-0434-01. Historical anchors use original full monthly releases of this same real, seasonally adjusted series, with their published one-decimal growth rates. The registered resolver instead preserves the precision of the first-release level transform. Advance estimates are excluded from the reference sample."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Statistics Canada's official 2026 release calendar, fetched through web search at https://www150.statcan.gc.ca/n1/release-diffusion/2026-eng.pdf, explicitly pairs the October 2026 GDP-by-industry reference period with December 23, 2026. This verifies the registered resolution date rather than inferring it from release cadence."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch original Statistics Canada GDP-by-industry releases for August through December 2025, using the official release URLs listed in sourceContext.",
        "result": "Original full monthly headline growth: August 2025 -0.3%, September 0.2%, October -0.3%, November 0.0%, December 0.2%. Respective release dates were October 31, November 28, December 23, 2025, January 30 and February 27, 2026. Later revised comparison values were not substituted."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch the original January, February and March 2026 GDP-by-industry releases dated March 31, April 30 and May 29, 2026.",
        "result": "Original full monthly all-industries growth was 0.1% in January 2026, 0.2% in February and -0.1% in March. All three releases identify Table 36-10-0434-01 and seasonally adjusted real GDP."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch the original April through July 2026 GDP-by-industry releases and inspect their sector descriptions.",
        "result": "Original full monthly growth was April 0.5%, May 0.3%, June 0.3% and July 0.0%. July construction grew 1.3%, utilities grew 1.7% and manufacturing contracted 0.9%. The July release separately reported an August advance estimate of 0.2%; that advance estimate is not a historical full-release print or a numerical model anchor."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "The outside-view base rate is the mean of twelve original monthly prints from August 2025 through July 2026: [-0.3, 0.2, -0.3, 0.0, 0.2, 0.1, 0.2, -0.1, 0.5, 0.3, 0.3, 0.0]. Their sum is 1.1 percentage points, giving mean growth of 0.091667%. Seven prints were positive, three negative and two zero. This reference class anchors the forecast before current conditions are considered."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "official.lookup",
        "call": "Fetch Statistics Canada's August Labour Force Survey, July retail and manufacturing releases, and the Bank of Canada's September 2 policy announcement.",
        "result": "August employment fell 42,000, or 0.2%, while unemployment remained 6.4%. July retail sales volumes fell 1.1% and constant-dollar manufacturing sales fell 1.4%. The Bank of Canada held the overnight target at 2.25%. Its announcement described new US tariffs and Canadian countermeasures, tighter financial conditions and continuing energy-price pressures."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Level: the GDP level receives no mechanical growth adjustment. Momentum: April-July full-release growth averaged 0.275%, supporting a limited positive update. One-off effects: April's production recovery, June's census and World Cup activity, and July's weather-related utilities strength warrant tempering extrapolation. Policy mechanism: the rate hold supplies no new easing impulse; tariffs and tighter financing can restrain exports, investment and consumption. August employment softening adds a small negative adjustment. July retail and manufacturing weakness corroborates the July slowdown and is not counted again as a separate numerical deduction."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: Use a mean-reverting reference-class model anchored on the twelve fetched original monthly GDP growth prints. The prior is 1.1/12 = 0.091667%. A judgmental momentum weight of 0.25 gives 0.25*(0.275-0.091667) = +0.045833 percentage points; subtract 0.020 for temporary boosts, 0.060 for tariff and financing mechanisms, and 0.010 for employment softening, with zero incremental rate-hold adjustment. These adjustments are model judgments, not agency statistics. The resulting point is 0.047500%, rounded to 0.05%. Because this is a change series, compute dispersion from the growth values themselves: sum(x^2) = 0.75; sum((x-mean)^2) = 0.75-12*(0.091667^2) = 0.649167; sigma = 0.242930 percentage points = sqrt(0.649167/11). The normal-approximation 80% predictive half-width is 1.28*sigma = 0.310951 percentage points. Widen by 1.10 for new tariff uncertainty and the unobserved intervening months, giving 0.342046. Around 0.05%, implied bounds are -0.292046% and 0.392046%; outward rounding to two decimals yields -0.30% and 0.40%."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Upside risk: sustained construction growth and a broad manufacturing and mining rebound could overcome tariff effects; simultaneous strength across these sectors would land above the interval at more than 0.40%. Downside risk: tariff-related order losses combined with refinery or mining outages and weaker consumption could produce growth below -0.30%, outside the interval. A temporary sector rebound alone would not establish persistent aggregate momentum."
      },
